---
title: OpenSchema
description: Shared TOML data contracts that let apps read and write the same structured data on a user's account.
---

OpenSchema is how structured, **queryable** data is described on NeoWorks. Each schema is a
small TOML contract — a named, versioned shape that any app can read or write, with the user's
permission. Because apps share schemas, the contacts your app sees are the same contacts the
user's other apps see.

Structured data is stored in a per-user database with row-level isolation. Unlike
[media](/docs/neoworks/storage), it is **not** end-to-end encrypted — it has to be plaintext server-side
so it can be filtered, sorted, and joined.

## Defining a schema

```toml
[table.tasks]
columns = [
  { name = "title",   type = "string"   },
  { name = "due_at",  type = "datetime" },
  { name = "contact", type = "string"   },
]
indexes = [
  { columns = ["user_id", "due_at"] }
]
```

## First-party schemas

These ship with NeoWorks and back the example apps:

- [Contacts](/docs/neoworks/openschema/contacts) — people: name, email, phone, avatar.
- [Calendar](/docs/neoworks/openschema/events) — events, recurrence, availability.
- [Tasks](/docs/neoworks/openschema/tasks) — tasks with due dates, notes, subtasks.

## Versioning

Schemas are versioned. Additive changes bump the minor version; breaking changes bump the
major and publish a new contract apps can migrate to.

